    "body": "Nyrstar zinc ingots. Credit: Nyrstar\nBy: Staff Writer November 27, 2026\nZinc and lead producer Nyrstar is to halt production at its Budel zinc smelter in the Netherlands on Nov. 30 as rising energy costs make the operation unprofitable.\nThe smelter in the southeastern Netherlands has an annual zinc production capacity of 315,000 tonnes and employs about 500 people. Nyrstar, whose operating business is owned by commodities trader Trafigura, said Thursday it’s suspending production to stem losses.\n“Market conditions for smelters remain extremely challenging, requiring policymakers to act quickly and decisively to retain strategic processing capacity,” Nyrstar CEO Matt Hadfield said in a news release .\nChinese smelting overcapacity, competition for concentrates and historically low treatment and refining charges are compounding the pressure of high energy costs on Western smelters. Nyrstar began a strategic review of Budel in September after the Dutch government omitted grid-cost relief for energy-intensive industries from its 2027 budget.\nThe company previously suspended production at Budel in January 2024 because of high energy costs and deteriorating market conditions. It announced a restart in April that year.\nShanghai Metals Market estimates the latest halt could cut refined zinc output by 200,000 to 220,000 tonnes if Budel remains idle throughout 2027.\nNyrstar expects no layoffs among permanent employees as a direct result of the halt and will maintain the site while it weighs its options. The company said in September it expected to complete the review by year-end. It hasn’t given a restart date.\nPrint\nCOMMODITY: Zinc-Lead\nREGION: Europe\nCOMPANY: Nyrstar Trafigura\nRepublish this article",
